.\" asciidraw manual. Check with: mandoc -Tlint asciidraw.1
.Dd November 22, 2026
.Dt ASCIIDRAW 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm asciidraw
.Nd draw ASCII mockups in the terminal with the mouse
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Ar file
.Nm
.Cm export
.Op Fl -font Ar n
.Op Fl -theme Ar light | dark
.Op Fl -pages Ar together | split
.Ar file output
.Nm
.Cm convert
.Ar file output
.Nm
.Fl v | -version
.Nm
.Fl h | -help
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is a drawing program for the terminal.
You drag the mouse to make boxes, ovals, lines and arrows, and click to type.
You can also place widgets such as buttons, checkboxes, input fields and
tabs.
Each shape stays separate, so you can move, resize, copy or delete it later.
.Pp
With no
.Ar file ,
.Nm
starts an untitled drawing.
A
.Pa .yaml
file opens as a drawing.
If it does not exist,
.Nm
starts a new drawing under that name.
A
.Pa .txt
or
.Pa .md
file comes in as one text shape: the first code block fenced with backticks,
or the whole file if there is none.
The first save then offers the same name with
.Pa .yaml .
.Pp
The terminal must pass mouse clicks and drags to programs.
.Pp
Two commands work without opening the drawing on screen.
Both read any
.Ar file
that
.Nm
opens, overwrite
.Ar output
without asking if it exists, and print the names of the files they wrote.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm export
Save the drawing as Markdown, PNG, JPEG, SVG or HTML.
The extension of
.Ar output
sets the format:
.Pa .md ,
.Pa .png ,
.Pa .jpg ,
.Pa .svg
or
.Pa .html .
It uses the export settings saved in the drawing, and the flags override
them for this run only.
.Fl -font
is the font size in pixels for every format but Markdown.
.Fl -theme
is
.Cm light
or
.Cm dark .
.Fl -pages Cm together
puts all the pages in one file.
.Fl -pages Cm split
writes one file per page, such as
.Pa out_page0.png .
Empty pages at the end get no file.
Put the flags before
.Ar file .
.It Cm convert
Save a drawing, a Markdown file or a text file as a drawing in
.Pa .adraw
or
.Pa .yaml .
.El
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl v , Fl -version
Print the version and exit.
.It Fl h , Fl -help
Print a short usage message and exit.
.El
.Sh USAGE
In this page,
.Sq ^T
means Ctrl+T.
Press
.Ic \&?
inside
.Nm
for a summary of the keys.
.Ss Drawing
.Bl -tag -width "right-drag"
.It drag
On empty space, draw with the current tool.
Shift while dragging a box or an oval makes it square or round.
.It click
On empty space, start typing.
Inside a box or an oval, type that shape's label.
With a widget tool, a click places the widget.
With the Erase tool, a click deletes.
.It right-drag
Delete every shape the rectangle touches, with any tool.
.It ^T
Open the menu of tools (Box, Oval, Line, Arrow, Fill, Erase, Select),
widgets and styles (ascii, line, round, double).
.It L
Open the library of ready-made pieces, with two tabs: builtin and custom.
Left and right switch tabs.
Up and down pick a piece.
Enter drops it where the mouse last was on the drawing, selected as one
group.
Esc closes the library.
The builtin tab has a login dialog, a register dialog, a browser window, a
web app with four panes and an open file dialog.
The custom tab reads
.Pa ~/.config/asciidraw/library/ ;
see
.Sx FILES .
.El
.Pp
A line goes sideways from where you press, turns once, then goes up or down
to where you let go.
Drag straight across or straight down for a line with no turn.
.Pp
To attach a line, start it in the empty cell just outside a shape, and end
it on another shape's edge or just outside it.
Don't start on the edge itself: pressing on a shape moves the shape.
Lines attach to boxes, widgets, and the rectangle around an oval.
An attached end follows its shape when the shape moves or changes size, and
the line picks its own turns.
.Pp
Where lines, box edges, window borders and tall button borders meet, they
join:
.Sq \[u253C] ,
.Sq \[u2524]
and
.Sq \[u252C]
in the line and round styles,
.Sq \[u256C]
in double, and
.Sq +
in ascii.
Oval curves, arrowheads and text don't join.
.Ss Typing
Enter starts a new line and Esc stops typing.
The arrow keys, Home and End move the cursor.
.Ss Selecting
Click a shape to select it, and Shift+click to add or remove one.
With the Select tool, drag a rectangle to select every shape fully inside it.
.Bl -tag -width "Tab, Shift+Tab"
.It v
Switch to the Select tool; press it again to go back.
.It ^A
Select everything.
.It Esc
Clear the selection.
.It Tab , Shift+Tab
Select the next or the previous shape.
.It g , ^G
Group the selected shapes, or ungroup them.
.El
.Ss The selection
When you are not typing, these keys act on the selected shapes.
Press Esc first if you are typing.
.Bl -tag -width "arrow keys"
.It drag
Move them.
With one shape selected, drag a box corner or either end of a line to
resize it.
A selected oval shows
.Sq +
where its corners would be.
.It arrow keys
Nudge them one cell.
.It y , ^C
Copy.
.It d , ^X
Cut.
.It p , ^V
Paste at the mouse.
.It x , Del
Delete, and leave the clipboard alone.
.It ^D
Duplicate.
.It \&] , \&[
Bring to the front, or send to the back.
.It t
Change the style: ascii, line, round, double.
On a fill, go to the next shade.
.It C
Open the color panel for text and background colors.
.It T
Open a small window with nine buttons, from top left to bottom right, to
choose where the label sits in a box or an oval.
The arrow keys move the label; Enter keeps it.
.It a
Line up or spread out the selected shapes.
Press a second key:
.Cm h , l , k
or
.Cm j ,
or an arrow key, lines up the left, right, top or bottom edges.
.Cm c
lines up the centers, so the shapes stack in a column.
.Cm m
lines up the middles, so the shapes sit in a row.
.Cm x
and
.Cm y
space the shapes out evenly from left to right, or from top to bottom.
Lining up needs two shapes; spacing out needs three.
.It Enter
Edit the label or the text.
.It Space
Check or uncheck a checkbox or radio button, or make the next tab active.
On a progress bar or a scrollbar, add 10; after 100 it goes back to 0.
On a fill, go to the next of its eight shades.
.El
.Ss Pages
Each page has a one-character name:
.Ql 0
to
.Ql 9 ,
then
.Ql a
to
.Ql z ,
then
.Ql A
to
.Ql Z ,
for 62 pages at most.
The bottom right corner shows five names at a time.
The current page is yellow.
Names of pages not made yet are dim, so you can see where the next page
will go.
As you move, the names slide along.
Click a bright name to go to that page.
Each page has its own undo.
.Pp
These keys work with or without a selection.
.Bl -tag -width "> or \&. (period)"
.It > or \&. (period)
Go to the next page.
On the last page, start a new, empty page, unless the last page is
already empty.
.It < or \&, (comma)
Go to the page before.
.It +
Copy the current page to a new page after it, and go to the copy.
.El
.Ss Files and view
.Bl -tag -width "PgUp, PgDn"
.It ^S
Save the drawing as YAML.
.It ^E
Export the drawing in each format ticked in the export settings:
Markdown, PNG, JPEG, SVG and HTML.
Only Markdown is ticked at first.
For
.Pa mock.yaml ,
with every format ticked, it writes
.Pa mock.md ,
.Pa mock.png ,
.Pa mock.jpg ,
.Pa mock.svg
and
.Pa mock.html
in the same folder.
It asks for a name if the drawing has none.
It asks before it replaces a file it did not write earlier in the same
session.
Exporting does not save the drawing.
.It S
Open the export settings.
Pick the formats, the font size (12 pixels by default), a
light or dark theme, and whether a drawing of several pages goes in one
file or in one file per page, such as
.Pa mock_page0.png .
Enter keeps the changes and Esc throws them away.
If you're typing text, press Esc first.
The settings are saved with the drawing.
.It ^N
Start a new drawing.
.It ^Q
Quit.
With unsaved changes, ^N and ^Q warn you first; press the key again to go
ahead.
.It ^Z , ^Y
Undo, redo.
.It ^O
Show or hide the panel.
.It wheel
Scroll up and down; with Shift, sideways.
.It PgUp , PgDn
Scroll half a window.
.It Home
Go back to the top left.
.It \&?
Show the key summary.
.El
.Sh CLIPBOARD
Copying also puts a picture of the selection on the system clipboard as
plain text.
Paste checks the system clipboard first.
If it holds text from another app, that text becomes a new text shape at the
mouse, or goes in at the cursor if you are typing.
Otherwise paste uses the shapes last copied in
.Nm .
.Pp
On macOS,
.Nm
uses
.Xr pbcopy 1
and
.Xr pbpaste 1 .
On Windows and in WSL, it uses PowerShell.
On Linux and the BSDs, it uses
.Xr wl-copy 1
and
.Xr wl-paste 1
under Wayland, or
.Xr xclip 1
or
.Xr xsel 1
under X11.
.Pp
Over SSH, or with none of those programs, copy uses the OSC 52 escape code,
which asks the terminal to set its clipboard.
Most modern terminals honor it.
In
.Xr tmux 1 ,
turn on
.Ql set -g set-clipboard on .
.Nm
can't read the clipboard this way, so paste uses the shapes last copied in
.Nm .
If a clipboard program fails to copy, copy falls back to OSC 52 too.
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width "WAYLAND_DISPLAY"
.It Ev SSH_TTY , Ev SSH_CONNECTION
If either is set, copy uses OSC 52, so it reaches the clipboard of the
machine in front of you.
This comes before every other choice.
.It Ev WSL_DISTRO_NAME
If set, and
.Pa powershell.exe
is on the
.Ev PATH ,
use it for the Windows clipboard.
.It Ev WAYLAND_DISPLAY
If set, use
.Xr wl-copy 1
and
.Xr wl-paste 1
when both are installed.
.It Ev DISPLAY
If set, and Wayland's programs weren't found, use
.Xr xclip 1
if it is installed, or else
.Xr xsel 1 .
.It Ev XDG_CACHE_HOME
On Linux and the BSDs, recovery copies go under this folder instead of
.Pa ~/.cache .
.It Ev XDG_CONFIG_HOME
If set, the custom library is
.Pa $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/asciidraw/library/
instead of
.Pa ~/.config/asciidraw/library/ .
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa ~/.config/asciidraw/library/
Pieces for the custom tab of the library, one per file.
.Nm
makes the folder the first time you open the library.
An
.Pa .adraw
or
.Pa .yaml
file is a drawing, and the piece is its first page.
A
.Pa .txt
file is one text shape with the whole file in it.
An
.Pa .md
file is one text shape with its first code block fenced with backticks,
or the whole file if it has none.
The file name, with dashes turned into spaces, names the piece, and a
number in front sets the order.
.El
.Pp
Every 30 seconds,
.Nm
writes unsaved changes to a recovery copy.
The next time you open the same drawing, it loads the copy and says so.
Saving deletes the copy, and so does throwing the changes away with ^Q or ^N
pressed twice.
The copies live in the user cache folder:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa ~/Library/Caches/asciidraw/recover/
on macOS
.It Pa ~/.cache/asciidraw/recover/
on Linux and the BSDs
.It Pa %LocalAppData%\easciidraw\erecover\e
on Windows
.El
.Pp
If there is no cache folder,
.Nm
uses the system's temporary folder instead.
A named drawing's copy takes its full path, with each slash turned into
.Sq % .
An untitled drawing's copy is
.Pa untitled.yaml ,
and comes back the next time you start
.Nm
with no file.
Two untitled sessions at once share that copy, and the last one to write it
wins.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
.Cm export
and
.Cm convert
exit with 2 for a wrong extension or a bad flag.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Start a new drawing called
.Pa login.yaml :
.Pp
.Dl asciidraw login.yaml
.Pp
Bring in the first code block of a Markdown file:
.Pp
.Dl asciidraw notes.md
